# Shell scripts must keep LF endings even on Windows checkouts.
#
# They run inside Linux containers, where /bin/sh is dash. A CRLF file makes dash read
# line 1 as `set -u<CR>` and abort with "set: Illegal option -" before a single test runs.
# Windows sets core.autocrlf=true by default, which rewrites text files to CRLF on
# checkout — so without this, the integration harness is broken on any Windows clone.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning: these are read by Linux tooling inside the images.
Dockerfile* text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Bind-mounted into the containers and parsed there, so it is in the same class as the scripts
# even though it is data. `awk '{print $2}'` over a CRLF line yields `7<CR>`, which is not an
# integer to `[ -lt ]` — the test errors, the shell takes the else branch, and the else branch is
# the one that reports the ratchet satisfied. Every other file mounted by `run.sh` and `ci.yml`
# was already covered by `*.sh`; this one was the exception, on a repo whose Windows clones get
# core.autocrlf=true.
scripts/lifecycle-floor.txt text eol=lf
